&lt;p&gt;React 19 removed &lt;code&gt;findDOMNode&lt;/code&gt;. Search your &lt;code&gt;src&lt;/code&gt; folder for it and you will probably find nothing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then I installed one very popular animation package in an empty folder and searched again:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm install react-transition-group@4.4.5 react@18 react-dom@18
grep -rn &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"findDOMNode"&lt;/span&gt; node_modules/react-transition-group/cjs/*.js
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;node_modules/react-transition-group/cjs/ReplaceTransition.js:106:      var maybeNode = child.props.nodeRef ? undefined : _reactDom.default.findDOMNode(this);
node_modules/react-transition-group/cjs/Transition.js:276:    var _ref2 = this.props.nodeRef ? [appearing] : [_reactDom.default.findDOMNode(this), appearing],
node_modules/react-transition-group/cjs/Transition.js:314:    var maybeNode = this.props.nodeRef ? undefined : _reactDom.default.findDOMNode(this); // no exit animation skip right to EXITED
node_modules/react-transition-group/cjs/Transition.js:378:    var node = this.props.nodeRef ? this.props.nodeRef.current : _reactDom.default.findDOMNode(this);
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Four calls. 4.4.5 is the latest release (npm, Nov 21, 2026), and its peer range is &lt;code&gt;react-dom &amp;gt;=16.6.0&lt;/code&gt;, so npm installs it next to React 19 without a word. Then, in the same folder:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm install react@19 react-dom@19
node -e &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"console.log(require('react-dom/package.json').version, typeof require('react-dom').findDOMNode)"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;19.3.1 undefined
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Your code is clean. Your dependency calls a function that no longer exists. (To be fair to the library: every one of those calls sits behind a &lt;code&gt;nodeRef&lt;/code&gt; check, so passing &lt;code&gt;nodeRef&lt;/code&gt; keeps you off that path. If you know to pass it.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I build Since, which tells you when something your code depends on changes in a way that breaks it. This week we taught it to look inside dependencies, not just at your own lines. It took three wrong answers before it gave a right one. Here they are.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. The blind spot we had
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Until this week, Since read your code. If your file called a function that a new release removed, you heard about it, at your line.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That covers most breaks. It misses the ones where you are two hops away: your code imports a component, the component calls the removed function, and the break shows up at runtime in a file you never opened. A changelog will not help you there either. React's release notes say &lt;code&gt;findDOMNode&lt;/code&gt; is gone. They do not say which of the hundreds of packages in your &lt;code&gt;node_modules&lt;/code&gt; still call it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the plan was simple to say: read what each dependency's own code calls, the same way we read a repository, and when an app resolves a version of something that removed that call, say so, with the file and line inside the dependency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Wrong answer one: the type that came back
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first live public scan with this turned on was a real Electron app, KRSHH/Sonosano. It said:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;@emotion/react 11.14.0 calls react's ElementType: ElementType was removed in react 16.0.0. This resolves react 19.1.1.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At first glance that's the feature working. Except &lt;code&gt;ElementType&lt;/code&gt; is right there in React 19's types.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both were true. React's type definitions really did drop &lt;code&gt;ElementType&lt;/code&gt; in 16.0.0. And then it came back: the same records show its signature changing in 18.0.0 and again in 19.0.0. You can't change the signature of something that doesn't exist.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We had only been reading removals and renames. A removal is a fact about one release. It is not a fact about now. The fix was to ask one more question before saying anything: is this removal still the last thing that happened to this symbol at the version you actually have? If anything later shows the symbol alive, the removal doesn't count.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Wrong answer two: the package that was written after the change
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With that rule in, the same scan still said @emotion/react's &lt;code&gt;FC&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;ForwardRefExoticComponent&lt;/code&gt; break on React 19, "removed in react 16.3.0."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Look at @emotion/react's peer range: &lt;code&gt;react &amp;gt;=16.8.0&lt;/code&gt;. It was written for a React that had already made that change. A package built on the new API can't be broken by the move to the new API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So a call now only counts against a dependency if the range it declares lets in a release from before the change. If every version you could ever pair it with already has the change, it's not that package's problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Wrong answer three: matching by the last word
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This one a review caught before it shipped, which is the only reason I can tell it calmly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first draft matched a dependency's call to a removal by the last part of the name. React 19 removed the old root &lt;code&gt;render&lt;/code&gt;. Lots of code calls &lt;code&gt;createRoot(el).render(...)&lt;/code&gt;, which is the new API and is fine. Same last word, opposite meaning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The match now needs the recorded path of what was removed, or a real tail of it, not just the last segment. It is stricter than what we do for your own code, on purpose: inside a dependency, nobody reads the code after the match, so the match has to be right on its own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  5. The part that was just slow
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sonosano's lockfile resolves 722 packages. The first scan had read what 8 of them call after 20 minutes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nothing was broken. Each scan asked for 50 versions to be read, and the next batch only went out when someone called again. At that pace a big lockfile needed about fifteen calls to be read in full. Now a scan asks for the whole tree at once, with the app's direct dependencies first so they never wait behind the long tail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And one thing is still not fixed, so I'll say it: a public scan keeps its first 100 findings. On Sonosano, the react-transition-group finding was sorted behind 26 Electron ones and cut. It's on the list.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  6. The right answer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today I scanned a public karaoke app, bhj/KaraokeEternal. Among its findings:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;react-transition-group 4.4.5 calls react-dom's findDOMNode (esm/ReplaceTransition.js:92): Removes ReactDOM.findDOMNode in react-dom 19.0.0. This resolves react-dom 19.3.1.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And it's placed at the app's own lines, the two files that import the library (&lt;code&gt;PlayerQR.tsx&lt;/code&gt; line 4 and &lt;code&gt;UpNow.tsx&lt;/code&gt; line 2), because that is where a person can do something about it. The same scan found rc-util 5.44.4 calling &lt;code&gt;findDOMNode&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;unmountComponentAtNode&lt;/code&gt;, which React 19 also removed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The break is in &lt;code&gt;node_modules&lt;/code&gt;. The line we show you is yours.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I'd tell myself before
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A removal is history, not state.&lt;/strong&gt; "X was removed in 16.0.0" says nothing about 19.3.1 until you check that nothing brought it back.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Who wrote the code matters as much as what it calls.&lt;/strong&gt; A package that was built after a change can't be broken by it. Its declared range tells you when it was built for.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The less a human reviews a finding, the stricter its match has to be.&lt;/strong&gt; Same last word is not the same function.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of these were hard once we saw them. All three were invisible until a real repo showed them to us. I don't think we would have found any of them with test fixtures we wrote ourselves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to see what your own &lt;code&gt;node_modules&lt;/code&gt; calls, a public repo scan on &lt;a href="https://since.dev?utm_source=devto" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Since&lt;/a&gt; needs no sign-in.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;What's the strangest break you've traced back to a dependency's dependency?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I'm Nabil. I build &lt;a href="https://since.dev?utm_source=devto" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Since&lt;/a&gt;, which tracks what your code depends on and tells you before it breaks.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first dark mode toggle I built in React, I built the way I had built them in plain JavaScript for years. I grabbed the document body and called &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;classList.toggle('dark')&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt; on it, and it worked. Click, dark. Click again, light. It shipped, and then a user reported the toggle losing its setting. They would switch to dark, click anything that re-rendered the page, and the page snapped back to light while the button still said dark. The class and the state had disagreed, and the class lost, because React re-renders from state, not from anything painted onto the DOM by hand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rule: in React, a class is not something you apply, it is something state decides. The button does not change its own class, it changes a value, and the render picks the class that matches. Treat &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;className&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt; as a function of state, and every toggle, tab bar, and theme picker gets simpler, because there is only ever one thing to update.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  the boolean version
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The simplest case is one button with two looks, and a boolean in &lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useState" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; is all the state it needs. The &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;className&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt; reads the boolean with a ternary, and the label reads the same boolean, so the look and the words can never disagree.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ThemeToggle.tsx&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ThemeToggle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dark&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setDark&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'button'&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setDark&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dark&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'btn btn-dark'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'btn'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dark&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Switch to light'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Switch to dark'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The button never touches the DOM itself. &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;setDark&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt; flips the boolean, React re-renders, and the &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;className&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt; expression picks the other string. The label changes in the same render, which is the part I like most. Two things that used to drift apart, the style and the text, now come from the same value, so they stay in sync for free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  the multi-way switch
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A boolean only gives you two looks. A theme picker, a layout switcher, or a tab bar has three or more, so the state holds a string instead, and the class comes from a small lookup. This is the version to reach for most often, because adding a fourth option means adding one line to the lookup and one button, and nothing else changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ThemePicker.tsx&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;themes&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;berry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'rounded-xl p-5 bg-[#C70D52] text-white'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;forest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'rounded-xl p-5 bg-[#2F6B4F] text-white'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;midnight&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'rounded-xl p-5 bg-[#1e1f26] text-white'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Theme&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;keyof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;typeof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;themes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ThemePicker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;theme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setTheme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Theme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'berry'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setPop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cardClass&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;themes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;theme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pop&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;' ring-4 ring-amber-300 shadow-lg'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;''&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;cardClass&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Pick&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;theme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;The&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;card&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;changes&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;because&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;the&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;changed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;keys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;themes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Theme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'button'&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTheme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;theme&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'swatch swatch-active'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'swatch'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'button'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setPop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pop&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Remove the pop'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Make it pop'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Try it: the theme picker, live&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Try it live on &lt;a href="https://nayemalam.com/blog/switch-classes-on-button-press" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nayemalam.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pick a theme, then hit make it pop. The line under the card shows the exact &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;className&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt; string the render produced, so you can watch the state turn into a class in real time. Nothing in the demo touches the DOM directly. Every change is a &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;setTheme&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt; or a &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;setPop&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt;, and the class follows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  the shortcut that invents a class named false
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The version that bites people is the shortcut. Template literals make it tempting to write the condition with &lt;code&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/code&gt;, and it looks right until you open devtools and find a class literally named false. When dark is false, the expression evaluates to false, the template literal turns it into the string "false", and the button gets a class that matches nothing in your CSS. It does nothing visible, which is why it survives in codebases for months, and it confuses the next person who inspects the element.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;the-false-class.ts&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// The shortcut: when dark is false, this renders class="btn false".&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;withShortcut&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`btn ${dark &amp;amp;&amp;amp; 'btn-dark'}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// The ternary: the class list only ever holds real class names.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;withTernary&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`btn ${dark ? 'btn-dark' : ''}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The ternary is two characters longer and only ever holds real class names, so it is the version to write by default. The &lt;code&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/code&gt; shortcut has a safe home, though, and it is the next section.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  a tiny helper for when the list grows
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once a component has three or four conditional classes, nested ternaries in the JSX get hard to read, and that is when the join belongs in a tiny helper. It takes the class names, drops the false ones, and joins what is left with spaces. Six lines, reused across projects, and it is the place where &lt;code&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/code&gt; becomes safe, because false values disappear before they reach the DOM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;cx.ts&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;cx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;parts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;undefined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;parts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;' '&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;classes&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;cx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'btn'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dark&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'btn-dark'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;large&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'btn-large'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The rule is simple. The base class stays outside the condition, always on, and only the switching part is conditional. A boolean gets a ternary, three or more options get a lookup, and a long list gets the helper. The DOM is left alone, because the moment a class is painted by hand, the next render wipes it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The class is a render of state, not a thing you apply. When a button looks wrong, check the state first, because the DOM only shows what state said. A ternary for two looks, a lookup for three or more, a helper when the list grows, and the class follows the state every time.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally posted on &lt;a href="https://nayemalam.com/blog/switch-classes-on-button-press" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nayemalam.com/blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The SeatFinder check-in button lives in the guest row. It is a small thing, one button per guest, and the checked-in state looks like it belongs right there next to it, inside the row component. Put it there and the button works, it flips to Checked in on tap, while the header count says 0 of 80 where it should say 3 of 80. The row knew. The parent had no idea. (The row knew, the parent had no idea, and that gap was the bug.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tempting next move is worse: change the prop directly from the child, as if the row could reach up and rewrite what the parent passed down. React ignores it. Props are read-only by design, and a child that mutates them is writing on a copy that nobody re-reads. One-way data flow is not a diagram in the docs, it is the rule that decides where state is allowed to live.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix has a name in the React docs, &lt;a href="https://react.dev/learn/sharing-state-between-components" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;lifting state up&lt;/a&gt;, and it is simpler than it sounds. The state moves into the closest parent that needs to see it, and the parent passes a callback down to the child. The child calls the callback when something happens, the parent updates the state, and the new value flows back down as a prop. Props go down, callbacks go up, and the circle is complete.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  the version that did not work
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is that version, stripped down so the shape is easy to see. The state lives in the row, the button works, and the parent is blind.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GuestRow.tsx&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Guest&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;GuestRow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Guest&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;checked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setChecked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'button'&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setChecked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;disabled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;checked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;checked&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Checked in'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Check in'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;SeatingChart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;guests&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;guests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;GuestRow&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This compiles, it runs, and the button even works. The problem is the boundary. The checked-in state belongs to the row, so nothing outside the row can read it. The header count, the progress bar, the export button that needs the checked-in list, all of them are locked out. State that lives in the wrong component is not broken, it is just unreachable, and unreachable state gets duplicated. The usual workaround is a second copy of the same state in the parent, just to feed the header, and then the two copies drift apart, because nothing keeps them in sync.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  the fix: lift the state into the parent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is one move: take the &lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useState" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; call out of the row and put it in the parent. The row keeps the button, it just loses the state. The parent owns the state and hands the row two props, the current value and a callback to ask for a change. When the button is tapped, the row calls &lt;code&gt;onCheckIn&lt;/code&gt;, the parent updates its state, React re-renders, and the new checked value flows back down into the row as a prop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SeatingChart.tsx&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Guest&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;GuestRow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;checked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onCheckIn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;checked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;onCheckIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'button'&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;onCheckIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;disabled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;checked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;checked&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Checked in'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Check in'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;SeatingChart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;guests&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;guests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;checkedIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setCheckedIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;values&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;checkedIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;checked&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;GuestRow&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;checked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;checkedIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;onCheckIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
              &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCheckedIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;guest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Read it as a loop. The parent renders the row with a &lt;code&gt;checked&lt;/code&gt; prop and an &lt;code&gt;onCheckIn&lt;/code&gt; prop. The row renders the button. A tap calls the callback. The callback updates the parent state. The parent re-renders and passes the new checked value down. Nothing reaches up, nothing reaches across. Every change travels the same road, down as data, up as a callback, and that is what makes the flow easy to trace when something goes wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  name the prop after the event
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Name the callback prop after the event, &lt;code&gt;onCheckIn&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;onToggle&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;onSelect&lt;/code&gt;, not after the setter. Passing &lt;code&gt;setCheckedIn&lt;/code&gt; straight down as a prop works, and for a tiny example it is fine, but the wrapper reads better once the parent has anything to decide. Maybe checking in also stamps a time, or skips guests who already checked in somewhere else. The wrapper is where that logic lives, and the child stays simple about it, which is exactly what you want from a row component. The child knows a button was pressed. The parent decides what that means.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Try it: the button lives in the child, the count lives in the parent&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Try it live on &lt;a href="https://nayemalam.com/blog/change-parent-state-from-child" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nayemalam.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tap a few guests in and watch the header count move. The buttons live in the child rows, the count lives in the parent, and the only connection between them is the &lt;code&gt;onCheckIn&lt;/code&gt; callback. Reset clears the parent state, and every row follows, because the rows only ever render what the parent passes down.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  the stale-state trap
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One more trap lives in the callback itself. A state update that reads the current value can go stale when two updates run in the same handler. The symptom is a button that adds one when the handler asks for two. The bug is stale state: both updates read the same value, so the second overwrites the first instead of building on it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;DoubleCounter.tsx&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;DoubleCounter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;addTwo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'button'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;addTwo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Add&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;two&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Both calls read the same &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt;, because React batches updates from the same event and the state variable stays the same between the two lines. The fix is the functional form, where each update gets the latest value instead of the stale one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;DoubleCounter.tsx&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;addTwo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The functional form is the default any time the new state depends on the old state, which in practice means most callbacks a child calls. It is a small habit and it removes a whole class of off-by-one bugs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  when two children need the same state
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same move scales up. When two siblings need the same state, a search box and a list, a filter and a count, the state lifts into their shared parent, and both children get props from the same source. That is still the same pattern, one owner, values down, callbacks up. If the state keeps climbing, past three or four levels of props, that is the point where React context starts earning its keep, but most of the time the shared parent is close by, and lifting one level is all it takes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;State has an address, and the address matters as much as the value. State in the child is invisible to the parent. State in the parent is visible to every child that needs it, through props, and changeable by any of them, through callbacks. Props go down, callbacks go up. Keep that boundary clean and the components stay small, the bugs stay easy to find, and the header says 3 of 80.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally posted on &lt;a href="https://nayemalam.com/blog/change-parent-state-from-child" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nayemalam.com/blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A bio on a wedding site, a long review on a poster page, a description on a dashboard card. Three places, one pattern: a boolean, a button, a slice of text. Each copy had drifted a little, different truncation lengths, different labels, one missing the collapse state entirely. That is the shape of a component that should exist once. When the same behavior shows up in three places, the question is not how to write it a fourth time, it is where it lives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is one component with a clear contract: it takes text and a line count, and it owns the boolean, the button, and the accessible state. Two details make it feel finished instead of hacked together: truncation by lines instead of characters, and a button that hides itself when there is nothing to expand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  the inline version
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The inline version is ten lines, and ten lines feel too small to abstract. A &lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useState" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; boolean, a slice of the text, and a button that flips it. It works fine the first time. The cost shows up on the second and third copy, when each one picks up its own quirks. One truncates at 100 characters, one at 150. One says Show more, one says Read more. One forgets the Read less state entirely, so the text grows and has no way back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;BioCard.tsx&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;BioCard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bio&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expanded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setExpanded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;preview&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;120&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expanded&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bio&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;preview&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'...'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'button'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setExpanded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expanded&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Read less'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Read more'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;There is also a quieter bug hiding in the slice. Cutting at 120 characters cuts mid-word, so the preview ends on a half word like "wedd...", and the ellipsis lands wherever the count ran out instead of at the end of a sentence. It reads like the page glitched, because it kind of did.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  one component, every bio
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reusable version moves the boolean inside a component and lets the caller pass the text and the number of lines. Truncation moves to CSS with &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/line-clamp" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;line-clamp&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, which clamps by lines instead of characters, so the text always ends cleanly at a line break and the browser adds the ellipsis for you. The button label and the &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;aria-expanded&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt; attribute both read the same boolean, so a screen reader hears the same state the sighted user sees.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ReadMore.tsx&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReadMoreProps&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;lines&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReadMore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lines&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReadMoreProps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expanded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setExpanded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;clampedStyle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CSSProperties&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expanded&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;display&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'-webkit-box'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nc"&gt;WebkitLineClamp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lines&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nc"&gt;WebkitBoxOrient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'vertical'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;overflow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'hidden'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;clampedStyle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'button'&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;aria&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expanded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expanded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setExpanded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expanded&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Read less'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Read more'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Every place that needed a toggle before now becomes one line. The bio, the review, and the description all pass their text in and get the same behavior, the same label, and the same accessible state. When the design changes, there is one file to change instead of three.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Usage.tsx&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReadMore&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./ReadMore'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReadMore&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;vendor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReadMore&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;review&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lines&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReadMore&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lines&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Try it: the read more toggle, live&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Try it live on &lt;a href="https://nayemalam.com/blog/reusable-read-more-toggle-button" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nayemalam.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The demo above is the component from this post, running with a real paragraph. Collapse it to three lines, expand it to the full text, and notice the button label and the expanded state change together. Nothing about it is special, and that is the point. A toggle that works the same everywhere is a toggle you stop thinking about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  the button should disappear when the text is short
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The one loose end is a short bio. If the text already fits in three lines, the Read more button sits there doing nothing, and clicking it changes nothing, which looks broken even though nothing is broken. The fix is a measurement: after render, compare the paragraph's &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;scrollHeight&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt; to its &lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;clientHeight&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt;, and only show the button when the full text is taller than the clamped box. Three additions to the same component, using &lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useRef" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, handle it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ReadMore.tsx&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;textRef&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;HTMLParagraphElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;clamped&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setClamped&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;textRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setClamped&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;scrollHeight&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;clientHeight&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lines&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Attach the ref to the paragraph, then render the button only when needed:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;clamped&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'button'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setExpanded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expanded&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Read less'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Read more'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The rule I use: a component earns its place the second time you need it, not the first. One boolean, one button, and one file to change when the design changes. The clamp handles the truncation, the button handles the state, and the measurement hides the button when there is nothing to expand. Small pieces like this are where a codebase stays calm, because the same behavior in ten places comes from one place.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally posted on &lt;a href="https://nayemalam.com/blog/reusable-read-more-toggle-button" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nayemalam.com/blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;useCallback is a React Hook that lets you cache (memoize) a function definition between re-renders. Instead of creating a brand-new function on every render, React returns the exact same function instance from memory unless one of its dependencies changes.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cachedFn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Your function logic here&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dependencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;• fn (First Argument): The function you want to cache.&lt;br&gt;
• dependencies (Second Argument): An array of reactive values (props, state, or variables declared inside the component) that the function relies on. If any value in this array changes, React recreates the function&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h5&gt;


  Add &lt;code&gt;data-source="path:line"&lt;/code&gt; to every JSX element in development, for Vite, Next.js and Babel. Zero runtime, works in any DevTools, and great with AI coding agents.
&lt;/h5&gt;




&lt;p&gt;It's your first day on a new project. You don't know the codebase at all, and your first task is to fix a button on the checkout page. You open DevTools, click the button and see this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight html"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"MuiButtonBase-root MuiButton-root css-1x2y3z4"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now find it in the code. You search for the class name: it's generated. You search for the text: it comes from translations. You search for the component name: it's used in 140 places. You follow imports from the router to the page, from the page to a layout, from the layout to a feature folder. Tens of minutes later, sometimes hours, you finally find the line you need.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I did exactly this for years, on every new project. Then a small Babel plugin turned up, and my everyday development changed for good: I stopped searching and started opening the right line straight away.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since then it has moved with me from project to project. But projects moved on to Next.js and Vite, where a Babel plugin doesn't fit, and I didn't want to go back to searching. So I wrote an SWC plugin for Next.js and Rspack and a native Vite plugin, with the same options and the same output. Now it covers the three main React toolchains.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What it does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The plugin adds a &lt;code&gt;data-source&lt;/code&gt; attribute with the file and line to every JSX element:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/components/Example.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"wrapper"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Pencil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;16&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DialogContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Hello&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DialogContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;becomes&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"wrapper"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"src/components/Example.tsx:2"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Pencil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;16&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"src/components/Example.tsx:3"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"src/components/Example.tsx:4"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DialogContent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"src/components/Example.tsx:5"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Hello&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DialogContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Inspect any node in the browser and the file and line are right there in the Elements panel. No extension, no runtime, no editor integration needed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why not React DevTools or a click-to-component tool?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those are great, and I use them too. A plain attribute has a few properties they don't:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It's just HTML.&lt;/strong&gt; It works in any browser, in Safari's Web Inspector, in a remote session, in &lt;code&gt;document.querySelector('[data-source^="src/pages/checkout"]')&lt;/code&gt;, in a Playwright trace or a saved HTML snapshot.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No runtime.&lt;/strong&gt; Nothing is injected into the bundle except the attribute strings, and you enable the plugin in development only, so production stays clean.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It doesn't depend on React internals.&lt;/strong&gt; React 19 stopped keeping the &lt;code&gt;__source&lt;/code&gt; location on elements and &lt;code&gt;_debugSource&lt;/code&gt; on fibers, which broke click-to-source tools; they had to switch to other mechanisms. An attribute added at build time doesn't care.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It's copyable.&lt;/strong&gt; QA can paste it into a bug report. You can paste it into a chat with an AI agent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The AI agent use case
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This turned out to be the main reason I use it today. When I work with Claude Code, Cursor or Copilot on UI, the hardest part of a prompt is often "which element do I mean". Now I inspect it, copy &lt;code&gt;data-source="src/features/cart/Summary.tsx:58"&lt;/code&gt;, and the agent opens the exact file and line. No guessing between three components with similar names, no "I've updated the wrong Button".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Setup
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Vite
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm install -D vite-plugin-react-source-string
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// vite.config.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"vite"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"@vitejs/plugin-react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reactSourceString&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"vite-plugin-react-source-string"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;command&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;plugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;command&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"serve"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;reactSourceString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It's a native Vite plugin written in TypeScript: it parses with oxc-parser, rewrites with magic-string (with source maps) and runs with &lt;code&gt;enforce: "pre"&lt;/code&gt;. No Babel, no WASM. It works with Vite 5 and later, including Rolldown-based Vite. Every Monday CI builds a test app with the plugin on Vite 5, 6, 7, the latest release and the current beta, so a breaking Vite release shows up before it reaches you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Next.js (SWC)
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm install -D swc-plugin-react-source-string@next-16
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// next.config.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nextConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;experimental&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;swcPlugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;NODE_ENV&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"development"&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"swc-plugin-react-source-string"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;root&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cwd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nextConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;SWC plugins are WASM and tied to the &lt;code&gt;swc_core&lt;/code&gt; version inside the bundler, so the package has dist-tags per bundler: &lt;code&gt;next-16&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;next-15&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;rspack&lt;/code&gt;. The README has the table. Every Monday a CI job checks for new Next.js and Rspack releases. When one ships with a new &lt;code&gt;swc_core&lt;/code&gt;, the job rebuilds the plugin, runs a real &lt;code&gt;next build&lt;/code&gt; with it and opens a pull request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Babel and Webpack
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm install -D babel-plugin-react-source-string
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// babel.config.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;exports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;api&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;plugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;api&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"development"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"babel-plugin-react-source-string"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Babel 7 and Babel 8 are supported. CI runs the tests against both, and against the newest &lt;code&gt;@babel/core&lt;/code&gt;, every week.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Options
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All three plugins share the same options and produce the same output:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it does&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;excluded&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Names to skip, e.g. &lt;code&gt;["Slot"]&lt;/code&gt;. Case-insensitive.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;root&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Project root for relative paths.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;libraries&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tag HTML elements plus only the components imported from these packages, e.g. &lt;code&gt;["@mui/material"]&lt;/code&gt;. Less noise in the props of your own components.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;preferCallSite&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;For wrappers: point to where a component is used, not to the wrapper. More below.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A reader's question that became a feature
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After my LinkedIn post about the plugins, a reader asked:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Does the Vite plugin handle components that pass props down to a UI library element, so the attribute points at your own file and not at node_modules?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The short answer was yes: the plugin never transforms &lt;code&gt;node_modules&lt;/code&gt;, so the attribute always points to your code. By default it points to the &lt;em&gt;innermost&lt;/em&gt; file in your code that renders the DOM node. Take a typical design-system wrapper:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/components/MyButton.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;MyButton&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;variant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"contained"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// line 2&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/pages/Checkout.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MyButton&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Pay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MyButton&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;                &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// line 42&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Both elements are tagged. &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;MyButton&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; gets &lt;code&gt;Checkout.tsx:42&lt;/code&gt; and passes it down through &lt;code&gt;{...props}&lt;/code&gt;, but inside the wrapper the plugin adds its own attribute &lt;em&gt;after&lt;/em&gt; the spread, so it wins. The DOM says &lt;code&gt;MyButton.tsx:2&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's right when you're working on &lt;code&gt;MyButton&lt;/code&gt;. But the question made me think about the other case: &lt;code&gt;MyButton&lt;/code&gt; is used 300 times, and what you actually want to know is &lt;em&gt;which page&lt;/em&gt; rendered this particular button.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So all three plugins now have an opt-in flag:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;reactSourceString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;preferCallSite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;With it, the plugin puts its attribute &lt;em&gt;before&lt;/em&gt; spread props, so a value coming from the parent wins and the DOM says &lt;code&gt;Checkout.tsx:42&lt;/code&gt;. It works through nested wrappers too: in &lt;code&gt;Page → Toolbar → MyButton → Button&lt;/code&gt;, where each one spreads its props, the outermost call site wins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Where it helps:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Design systems and UI kits.&lt;/strong&gt; "This is MyButton" tells you little. "This is the Pay button on the checkout page" tells you everything.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bug reports and QA.&lt;/strong&gt; The attribute leads to the page code that configures the element: its props, text and handlers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;AI agents.&lt;/strong&gt; The agent edits that one usage instead of changing a wrapper the whole app depends on.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's off by default, because when you work on the wrappers themselves you want to land in the wrapper. One limitation: a wrapper that picks props explicitly (&lt;code&gt;className&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;onClick&lt;/code&gt;) and doesn't spread them still points to itself, since the call-site value never reaches the DOM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A few implementation notes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The line is the line of the opening &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/code&gt;, so multi-line elements point to their start.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An element that already has &lt;code&gt;data-source&lt;/code&gt; is left alone. That's exactly what makes &lt;code&gt;preferCallSite&lt;/code&gt; possible.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fragments are never tagged: &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Fragment&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;React.Fragment&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; accept only &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;children&lt;/code&gt;, and React warns about any other prop.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Turbopack-style virtual paths like &lt;code&gt;[project]/src/a.tsx&lt;/code&gt; are normalized, so the output is the same across bundlers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Releases go through release-please and npm trusted publishing with provenance. No long-lived npm tokens.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Vite: &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/vite-plugin-react-source-string" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;npm&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;a href="https://github.com/tanchu/vite-plugin-react-source" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Next.js / Rspack (SWC): &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/swc-plugin-react-source-string" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;npm&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;a href="https://github.com/tanchu/swc-plugin-react-source" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Babel / Webpack: &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/babel-plugin-react-source-string" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;npm&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;a href="https://github.com/tanchu/babel-plugin-react-source-string" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;preferCallSite&lt;/code&gt; came from a single comment, so feedback really does shape these plugins. Issues and PRs are welcome, and if a plugin saves you time, a ⭐ on GitHub helps other people find it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Email verification code often starts with one promise: &lt;code&gt;sendVerificationEmail(email)&lt;/code&gt;. That shape is convenient, but it hides the decisions an application actually needs to make.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Did the provider accept the request? Was the message delayed? Did a retry create a second token? Is the address invalid, or is the inbox simply not ready yet? When every outcome becomes either &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt; or an exception, a React form has to guess. Those guesses turn into confusing spinners, duplicate requests, and tests that pass for the wrong reason.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I prefer a typed email adapter with an explicit failure contract. The adapter owns provider details, while the UI and the Node.js API consume stable application states. It is a small boundary, but it makes a verification flow much easier to ship and debug.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why the adapter should own failure states
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An email provider can expose dozens of errors. Your product usually needs only a few meaningful states:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;accepted:&lt;/strong&gt; the request was accepted and has a correlation ID;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;already pending:&lt;/strong&gt; the same user has an active verification attempt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;invalid recipient:&lt;/strong&gt; the address cannot receive the message;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;provider unavailable:&lt;/strong&gt; the request may be retried later;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;unknown failure:&lt;/strong&gt; the system should retain evidence and stop guessing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The adapter should also say what it do not know. An accepted response does not prove the user has received or opened the message. That distinction prevents a success toast from promising too much.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also gives the team a place to define policy. For example, a disposable email address generator may be useful in a local or integration test, but production onboarding might reject some temporary domains. The adapter can classify that decision without leaking provider-specific strings into every React component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For automated checks, pair the adapter with &lt;a href="https://dev.to/mrdapperx/cron-publishers-need-inbox-probes"&gt;inbox probes for automated checks&lt;/a&gt;. The probe can observe delivery, while the application contract remains focused on what it requested and what it can safely retry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Model the contract with a discriminated union
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a deliberately small TypeScript model:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationResult&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'accepted'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attemptId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expiresAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'already-pending'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attemptId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retryAfterSeconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'invalid-recipient'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reason&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'format'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'blocked-domain'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'provider-unavailable'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retryAfterSeconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'unknown-failure'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;referenceId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The useful bit is not the type itself. It is the fact that every caller must decide what each state means. A new result kind will create a compile error in the places that render or log it, which is a much better reminder than a new provider error appearing in production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The adapter can translate provider responses at one place:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;requestVerification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requestId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;emailProvider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;send&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;template&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'verify-account'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;requestId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;accepted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'accepted'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;attemptId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;messageId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;expiresAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expiresAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'RATE_LIMITED'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'provider-unavailable'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retryAfterSeconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'INVALID_RECIPIENT'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'invalid-recipient'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reason&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'format'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'unknown-failure'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;referenceId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;requestId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The real provider client will need a proper error boundary and timeout handling. The point is that the rest of the application should not need to know whether the provider called the condition &lt;code&gt;RATE_LIMITED&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;429&lt;/code&gt;, or something more odd. A stable contract are easier to document and test.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Keep React rendering separate from delivery logic
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The React component should translate the result into user action, not inspect raw provider responses:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationNotice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationResult&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;switch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'accepted'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Check&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;your&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inbox&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;the&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;verification&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;link&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'already-pending'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Another&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;link&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;is&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;again&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;moment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'invalid-recipient'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Check&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;the&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;address&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;again&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'provider-unavailable'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Email&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;is&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;busy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;right&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Please&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retry&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;soon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'unknown-failure'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;We&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;could&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;send&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;the&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;link&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Reference&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;referenceId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The UI are then free to choose whether a state is an inline error, a retry button, or a quiet status update. Keep the &lt;code&gt;attemptId&lt;/code&gt; out of user-facing copy, but log it with the request ID so support can trace one attempt without exposing the verification token.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is more safer than letting every component invent its own retry rules. A retry should reuse an idempotency key for the same user action. A genuinely new “send again” action should get a new request ID and a new attempt, with rate limits applied at the API boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the flow has OAuth or another stateful redirect, it also helps to define &lt;a href="https://dev.to/sophiax99/oauth-state-needs-a-replay-boundary-fkf"&gt;a replay boundary for stateful flows&lt;/a&gt;. Email verification and OAuth are different features, but both become fragile when a retry is treated as a brand-new action without a record of what already happened.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Use the same boundary in Node.js tests
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tests should exercise the adapter with a fake provider, not call a real inbox for every unit test. A useful fake records requests and lets the test select a response:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fakeProvider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createFakeEmailProvider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;accepted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;messageId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'msg-123'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expiresAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2030-01-01T00:15:00Z'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;requestVerification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'user-7'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'dev@example.test'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;requestId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'signup-7'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toMatchObject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'accepted'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attemptId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'msg-123'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fakeProvider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toBe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'signup-7'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Use an integration inbox only for a smaller set of delivery tests. Keep the test address and message evidence tied to the request ID, then clean it up after the assertion. If the message didn't arrived, the recorded provider response and correlation ID should still explain whether the bug was in the app, queue, or test fixture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some teams use fixture labels such as &lt;code&gt;tempail&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;dummy e mail&lt;/code&gt; while testing validation. That is fine as plain test data, but those strings should never become production domains or backlink anchors. A provider such as &lt;a href="https://tempmailso.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tempmailso&lt;/a&gt; can be useful for a controlled disposable mailbox workflow, but treat any temporary inbox as untrusted test infrastructure and avoid sending real personal data to it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A practical implementation checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before shipping the adapter, check that:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Provider errors are translated into a small, documented result union.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Every send request has an idempotency or correlation key.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React renders application states instead of provider error codes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Retries distinguish the same action from an intentional new attempt.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Logs retain a reference ID without logging verification tokens.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Unit tests cover delayed, rejected, duplicate, and unknown outcomes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Integration tests prove delivery with isolated inboxes and cleanup.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This boundary keeps the code pleasantly boring, which is a good thing. When a delivery problem happens, the logs becomes evidence instead of a pile of guesses. And when the provider changes, the rest of your React and Node.js code can keep shipping.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When learning React, I came across three important hooks: &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At first, I found them confusing because they all use dependency arrays.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But after understanding their purpose, I realized that each hook solves a different problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's understand them with simple examples.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. What is useEffect?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; is used to perform an action after a component renders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, when we want to update the browser tab title whenever a counter changes.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here, whenever &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; changes, &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; updates the browser tab title.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In simple words:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;useEffect is used to perform actions after rendering.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. What is useCallback?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; is used to remember a function between renders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's say we have a function that prints a message.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sayHello&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Hello"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sayHello&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Click&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;me&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here, React remembers the function instead of creating a new function on every render.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The function will still work when we click the button.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In simple words:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;useCallback remembers a function.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. What is useMemo?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; is used to remember the result of a calculation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, calculating the total price of products.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;price&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;quantity&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;price&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;quantity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;price&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;quantity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here, React calculates the total price and remembers the result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the dependencies do not change, React can reuse the previous result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In simple words:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;useMemo remembers a calculated value.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. What is the difference?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's imagine we are building a shopping application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; → Fetches products from an API.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; → Remembers a function that adds a product to the cart.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; → Remembers the total price of the cart.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The easiest way to remember these hooks is:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; → Do something.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; → Remember a function.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; → Remember a value.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All three hooks are useful in React, but they have different purposes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We don't need to use them in every component. We should use them when they solve a real problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each hook has its own purpose. Use only the hooks you need to make your component work better.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A child component's &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; reads something its parent's effect was supposed to set up. It's &lt;code&gt;undefined&lt;/code&gt;. You add a &lt;code&gt;console.log&lt;/code&gt; to both and find the child's effect ran &lt;strong&gt;first&lt;/strong&gt;, even though the parent rendered first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nothing is broken. That's the order React promises.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The mental model: down, then back up
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The server goes first, the browser second. Inside the browser, render walks &lt;strong&gt;down&lt;/strong&gt; the tree, parent before child. Effects walk back &lt;strong&gt;up&lt;/strong&gt;, child before parent. The screen updates in between.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Picture nested boxes. Rendering opens them from the outside in. Effects close them from the inside out, because a parent isn't finished until its children are.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fbitlyst.blog%2Fassets%2Frender-order-relay.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fbitlyst.blog%2Fassets%2Frender-order-relay.jpg" alt="AI-generated illustration. The server builds the page, the HTML travels, the browser shows it, and only then does the JavaScript switch it on." width="800" height="400"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The full order, from request to click
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;One page load in the App Router&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;1. Server Components run                   — Data fetching, async components. They never run in the browser
2. Client Components render on the server  — 'use client' components still render to HTML here. No effects, no state updates
3. HTML reaches the browser                — The user sees the page. Buttons don't respond yet
4. JavaScript loads                        — The bundle for your Client Components downloads
5. Client Components render again          — Hydration: same components, same props, matched against the HTML
6. DOM updated, refs attached              — React writes the DOM and hands you the elements
7. useLayoutEffect                         — Child first, then parent. The browser hasn't painted yet
8. Paint                                   — The user sees the result
9. useEffect                               — Child first, then parent. Runs after the screen updates
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;A Server Component always renders before the Client Components it passes props to. With streaming, a slow part behind Suspense can arrive after the rest of the page is already interactive.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On the server.&lt;/strong&gt; Server Components run first: they fetch data and pass it down as props. Then React renders your &lt;code&gt;"use client"&lt;/code&gt; components too, to turn them into HTML. That's the surprise for many people: a Client Component renders on the server. Only its effects and event handlers are skipped there. I ran a two-level tree through &lt;code&gt;renderToString&lt;/code&gt; and the log showed the &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; initializer, &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;, and both render functions. No effect of any kind ran.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In the browser.&lt;/strong&gt; The HTML is already on screen. When the JavaScript arrives, React renders the Client Components again (this is &lt;a href="https://bitlyst.blog/p/how-ssr-works-react-nextjs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;hydration&lt;/a&gt;), then runs the effects. Here's the hydration log from a &lt;code&gt;Parent&lt;/code&gt; that renders a &lt;code&gt;Child&lt;/code&gt;, on React 19.3.1:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Parent useState initializer
Parent useMemo
Parent render
Child render
Child ref attached
Child useLayoutEffect
Parent useLayoutEffect
Child useEffect
Parent useEffect
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Inside one render, hooks run in the order you wrote them. &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; run during the render itself. Effects only run after React has updated the DOM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When state changes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Calling &lt;code&gt;setCount(1)&lt;/code&gt; repeats the browser half, with one addition: every old effect is cleaned up before any new one runs. The log, with the &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; and ref lines left out:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Parent render (count=1)
Child render (props.count=1)
Child useLayoutEffect cleanup
Parent useLayoutEffect cleanup
Child useLayoutEffect
Parent useLayoutEffect
Child useEffect cleanup
Parent useEffect cleanup
Child useEffect
Parent useEffect
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The parent renders first, so the child receives the new props in the same pass. A state change re-renders that component and everything under it. Nothing above it runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  In practice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A parent's effect can't prepare something for a child's effect.&lt;/strong&gt; The child's runs first. Pass the value down as a prop, or create it during render.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Measure the DOM in &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt; It runs after the DOM exists and before paint, so a fix doesn't flash. Everything else goes in &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;. See &lt;a href="https://bitlyst.blog/p/useeffect-vs-uselayouteffect" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;useEffect vs useLayoutEffect&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Don't read &lt;code&gt;window&lt;/code&gt; during render in a Client Component.&lt;/strong&gt; It renders on the server, where &lt;code&gt;window&lt;/code&gt; doesn't exist. Read it in an effect.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fetch in a Server Component when you can.&lt;/strong&gt; It runs before any browser code, so the data is in the first HTML instead of arriving after an effect.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Honest stakes:&lt;/strong&gt; most components never notice the order. You meet it when two effects depend on each other, when a measurement flickers, or when code that touches &lt;code&gt;window&lt;/code&gt; crashes the server render.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Say it in an interview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"In the App Router, Server Components run first on the server and pass props down. Client Components also render on the server to produce HTML, but their effects don't run there. In the browser, React hydrates: it renders top-down, parent before child, updates the DOM and attaches refs, then runs layout effects bottom-up, child before parent, before paint, and regular effects bottom-up after paint. On a state update, the cleanups for the old effects run before the new effects."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cheat sheet
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Step&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Where&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Order&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Server Component&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Server only&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;First, parents before children&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Client Component render (&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;, JSX)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Server, then browser&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Parent before child&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Refs attached, &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Browser, before paint&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Child before parent&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Browser, after paint&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Child before parent&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;On update: cleanups&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Browser&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;All old cleanups before the new effects&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Server Components run first, only on the server.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Client Components render on the server too. Their effects don't run there.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Render goes down the tree. Effects come back up.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; runs before paint, &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; after it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On an update, old effects are cleaned up before new ones run.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Going deeper
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Advanced and optional. Everything above is enough to explain this in an interview. This part is for the curious: full numbers, edge cases and what's still unproven.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  How I logged the order
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React 19.3.1 and react-dom 19.3.1, under Bun. Every hook in a &lt;code&gt;Parent&lt;/code&gt; and a &lt;code&gt;Child&lt;/code&gt; pushed a line to one array. The server log came from &lt;code&gt;renderToString&lt;/code&gt;. The browser log came from &lt;code&gt;hydrateRoot&lt;/code&gt; on that same HTML inside a happy-dom document, wrapped in &lt;code&gt;act&lt;/code&gt; so effects flushed. The update log came from calling &lt;code&gt;setCount(1)&lt;/code&gt; inside &lt;code&gt;act&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A test document doesn't paint, so the logs can't show where paint happens. The "before paint" and "after paint" positions come from the React docs for &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Insertion effects run before everything
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useInsertionEffect&lt;/code&gt; ran right after render and before refs were attached. It exists for CSS-in-JS libraries that must add &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;style&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tags before any layout is read. You'll almost never write one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Strict Mode runs it twice in development
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In development, Strict Mode mounts each component, runs its cleanup, and mounts it again, so you see every effect run twice. That's on purpose: it checks your cleanups work. Production runs each effect once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Batching
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Several &lt;code&gt;setState&lt;/code&gt; calls in one event handler produce one render, not several. React 18 and later also batch updates inside promises and timeouts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Further reading
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/server-and-client-components" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Server and Client Components&lt;/a&gt;, Next.js docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useLayoutEffect" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;useEffect&lt;/a&gt;, React docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://bitlyst.blog/p/react-server-components-vs-use-client" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React Server Components vs "use client"&lt;/a&gt; on this blog&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://bitlyst.blog/p/react-nextjs-render-order" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Bitlyst&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A search box over a list. It works, and it shows up in code review every week:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filtered&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setFiltered&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setFiltered&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;List&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filtered&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Type a letter and the list updates. Nothing looks broken. But every keystroke renders &lt;code&gt;Results&lt;/code&gt; twice, and the first render shows the old list.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The mental model: a copy that runs late
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you can calculate it from props or state, it isn't state. Calculate it while rendering.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;filtered&lt;/code&gt; is a copy of an answer you already have. &lt;code&gt;items&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;query&lt;/code&gt; are the source. A copy kept in its own state has to be updated after the source changes, and until then it's out of date.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fbitlyst.blog%2Fassets%2Fderived-state-copies.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fbitlyst.blog%2Fassets%2Fderived-state-copies.jpg" alt="AI-generated illustration. The copy on the left trails behind its source. The view on the right reads the source directly, so it can't fall behind." width="800" height="400"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How the copy goes wrong
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Follow one keystroke through the code above:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;query&lt;/code&gt; changes, so React renders &lt;code&gt;Results&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;That render still reads the old &lt;code&gt;filtered&lt;/code&gt;, because the effect hasn't run yet. React puts the old list on screen.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;After the screen updates, the effect runs and calls &lt;code&gt;setFiltered&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;That state change renders &lt;code&gt;Results&lt;/code&gt; again, this time with the right list.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I counted this with React 19.3.1. Over 5,000 items, changing the query to &lt;code&gt;"item 42"&lt;/code&gt; rendered the effect version &lt;strong&gt;twice&lt;/strong&gt;. The first render still had all &lt;strong&gt;5,000&lt;/strong&gt; items. The second had the right &lt;strong&gt;111&lt;/strong&gt;. Steps 2 and 3 are the &lt;a href="https://bitlyst.blog/p/useeffect-vs-uselayouteffect" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;useEffect-after-paint gap&lt;/a&gt;: a slow device can show the stale list for a frame.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix deletes code:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;filtered&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;List&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filtered&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Same test, &lt;strong&gt;one&lt;/strong&gt; render, already showing 111 items. Nothing to keep in sync, because nothing was copied.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  In practice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same pattern hides behind other names:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// before: state + effect&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fullName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setFullName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setFullName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${first} ${last}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;last&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// after: a variable&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fullName&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${first} ${last}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Totals from a cart, &lt;code&gt;isValid&lt;/code&gt; from form fields, &lt;code&gt;selectedItem&lt;/code&gt; from a list plus an id: each one is a value you calculate while rendering, not a value you store.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the calculation is genuinely slow, wrap it in &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;. That still runs during the render, so there's no stale frame. It only skips the work when the inputs haven't changed:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;filtered&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Reach for an effect only when the code talks to something outside React: fetching, a subscription, a timer, the DOM. Copying one piece of React state into another isn't on that list.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Honest stakes:&lt;/strong&gt; with a short list you won't see the stale frame, and the extra render costs little. The real cost is bugs. Two copies of one answer drift apart: someone updates &lt;code&gt;items&lt;/code&gt; from a new place, forgets the effect's dependency list, and the screen disagrees with the data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Say it in an interview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"If a value can be computed from props or state, I compute it during render instead of storing it. Copying it into state with useEffect means the first render uses the old value, the effect runs after paint, and its setState causes a second render. Computing it inline gives one render with the right value. If the calculation is expensive I use useMemo. Effects are for syncing with things outside React, like the network or the DOM."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cheat sheet
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;You have&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Write&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A value computed from props or state&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A &lt;code&gt;const&lt;/code&gt; in the component body&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The same, but slow to compute&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; with its inputs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Data from the network&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An effect, or better, a data library&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A value the user edits&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If you can calculate it, it isn't state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; plus a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; that sets it gives two renders and a stale first frame.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Calculate the value in the component body. Use &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; only when it's slow.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Effects are for the world outside React, not for copying state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Going deeper
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Advanced and optional. Everything above is enough to explain this in an interview. This part is for the curious: full numbers, edge cases and what's still unproven.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  How I counted the renders
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React 19.3.1 and react-dom 19.3.1, rendered with &lt;code&gt;createRoot&lt;/code&gt; into a happy-dom document under Bun. Each component pushed a line to a log on every render. The list had 5,000 items named &lt;code&gt;item 0&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;item 4999&lt;/code&gt;. After mounting with an empty query, I re-rendered with &lt;code&gt;query="item 42"&lt;/code&gt; inside &lt;code&gt;act&lt;/code&gt;, which flushes effects:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Version&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Renders after the change&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What each render showed&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; + &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5,000 items, then 111&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Computed in the body&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;111 items&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both versions also mounted the same way: two renders for the effect version, one for the computed one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Resetting state when a prop changes
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A related case: state that the user does edit, but which should reset when an id changes, such as a comment draft per post. Don't reset it in an effect. Give the component a &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CommentBox&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;postId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;postId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;postId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A new &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; makes React treat it as a new component, so its state starts fresh, in the same render. See &lt;a href="https://bitlyst.blog/p/react-keys-reconciliation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;why React needs key&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Further reading
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;You Might Not Need an Effect&lt;/a&gt;, React docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://bitlyst.blog/p/react-useeffect-dependencies" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;useEffect dependencies&lt;/a&gt; on this blog&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://bitlyst.blog/p/react-derived-state-useeffect" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Bitlyst&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your React app looks perfect in the browser. Google sees an empty page.&lt;br&gt;
No rankings, no traffic, and Search Console just says "Crawled, currently not indexed" without telling you why.&lt;br&gt;
Here's how to see exactly what Google sees in 2 minutes, find the real cause and fix it with code tested on Next.js 16.4.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Why is Google not indexing my React or Next.js site?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Almost always, it's one of three problems:&lt;br&gt;
Rendering: your content only appears after JavaScript runs.&lt;br&gt;
Discovery: Google can't find your URLs (no real links, &lt;code&gt;/#/&lt;/code&gt; routes, no sitemap).&lt;br&gt;
Signals: canonicals, redirects, a stray &lt;code&gt;noindex&lt;/code&gt; or wrong status codes point Google away.&lt;br&gt;
If everything is technically fine and Google still says no, skip to the content section near the end.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  How can I see what Google actually sees on my page?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open View Source (not Inspect) or run a Live Test in Search Console's URL Inspection tool. If your main heading and text aren't in that HTML, Google may not be seeing them either.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;View Source, not Inspect. Press &lt;code&gt;Ctrl+U&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;Cmd+Option+U&lt;/code&gt; on Mac) and search for a sentence from your page. Inspect shows the page after JavaScript runs; View Source shows the raw HTML Google receives first.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check from the terminal:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Is your content in the raw HTML?&lt;/span&gt;
curl -s -A &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; Googlebot/2.1; +http://www.google.com/bot.html)"&lt;/span&gt; \
  https://yoursite.com/your-page &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; grep -i &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"your main heading"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Which status codes, redirects and robots headers does Google get?&lt;/span&gt;
curl -sIL https://yoursite.com/your-page &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; grep -iE &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"^HTTP|^location|x-robots-tag"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use URL Inspection's Live Test. In Search Console, click Test Live URL, then View Tested Page, and check the HTML and Screenshot tabs.
Content missing from the raw HTML? Start with rendering. Otherwise, skip to discovery and signals.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  What does my Search Console indexing status actually mean?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each status points to a different cause, so find yours (under Indexing &amp;gt; Pages) before changing any code.&lt;br&gt;
Discovered, currently not indexed: known but not crawled yet. Weak internal links, no sitemap or low priority.&lt;br&gt;
Crawled, currently not indexed: fetched, then rejected. Empty or thin HTML, near-duplicates or low quality.&lt;br&gt;
Duplicate without user-selected canonical: several URLs show the same content and none declares a canonical.&lt;br&gt;
Page with redirect: fine if intentional; a problem if your links or sitemap still point to it.&lt;br&gt;
Soft 404: the page returns &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; but looks empty or like an error.&lt;br&gt;
Excluded by 'noindex' tag: &lt;code&gt;noindex&lt;/code&gt; is in the HTML or the &lt;code&gt;X-Robots-Tag&lt;/code&gt; header.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Can Google index a client-side React app?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes, but Google must render your JavaScript in a separate step first, which adds delay and more ways to fail than serving ready-made HTML.&lt;br&gt;
A Create React App or Vite app sends crawlers an empty &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div id="root"&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and a script tag. Google crawls that shell, then queues the page for rendering in headless Chrome. Only then does it see your content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Did you know?&lt;/strong&gt; In a study of over 100,000 Googlebot fetches, Google fully rendered 100% of HTML pages. But while the median delay between crawling and rendering was 10 seconds, 10% of pages waited about 3 hours or more, and 1% waited about 18 hours.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Source: &lt;a href="https://vercel.com/blog/how-google-handles-javascript-throughout-the-indexing-process" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Vercel and MERJ, "How Google handles JavaScript throughout the indexing process" (2024)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Vercel builds Next.js and used nextjs.org data, so treat this as strong evidence, not the final word. The lesson holds: if rendering fails (a blocked API, a script error), Google indexes the empty shell.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Isn't Next.js SEO-friendly by default?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Only when content is rendered on the server. A &lt;code&gt;'use client'&lt;/code&gt; page that fetches data in &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; sends Google the same empty HTML as a plain React app.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/products/[slug]/page.tsx  (bad)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'use client'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`/api/products/${slug}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;setProduct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;slug&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;When we built this page on Next.js 16.4, the server HTML contained &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;p&amp;gt;Loading...&amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and no product. Fetch the data in a Server Component instead:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/products/[slug]/page.tsx  (good)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;notFound&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'next/navigation'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;getProduct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@/lib/products'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Props&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;slug&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ProductPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;slug&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getProduct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;slug&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;notFound&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now the content is in the HTML from the first byte. Keep interactive parts in small client components inside the server page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why are my titles and meta descriptions not showing in Google?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If JavaScript sets them after load, Google can see the same default title everywhere and treat pages as duplicates. Replace &lt;code&gt;react-helmet&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;document.title&lt;/code&gt; with the Metadata API:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Metadata&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'next'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;getPost&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@/lib/posts'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Props&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;slug&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;generateMetadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Metadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;slug&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getPost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;slug&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;excerpt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;alternates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;canonical&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`/blog/${slug}`&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This only works in Server Components. Recent Next.js versions may stream metadata to Googlebot on dynamic pages, so confirm your title in URL Inspection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Can ChatGPT and other AI tools see my React content?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mostly no, because most AI crawlers read only the raw HTML.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Did you know?&lt;/strong&gt; GPTBot, ChatGPT-User, OAI-SearchBot, ClaudeBot and PerplexityBot don't execute JavaScript. If your content only appears after React runs, these AI assistants likely can't see it. Google's Gemini is the exception, because it uses Googlebot's rendering. &lt;em&gt;(Data from December 2024.)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Source: &lt;a href="https://vercel.com/blog/the-rise-of-the-ai-crawler" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Vercel and MERJ, "The rise of the AI crawler"&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How does Google find the pages on my React site?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Through real &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;a href&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; links, your sitemap and external links. Google follows &lt;code&gt;href&lt;/code&gt; attributes; it doesn't click buttons.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Bad: no link in the HTML&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;router&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;push&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'/pricing'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Pricing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Good: renders &amp;lt;a href="/pricing"&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Link&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/pricing"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Pricing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Link&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Drop hash routes. To Google, &lt;code&gt;/#/pricing&lt;/code&gt; is just &lt;code&gt;/&lt;/code&gt;. Switch from &lt;code&gt;HashRouter&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;BrowserRouter&lt;/code&gt; and serve &lt;code&gt;index.html&lt;/code&gt; for every route.&lt;br&gt;
Generate a sitemap. In the App Router, &lt;code&gt;app/sitemap.ts&lt;/code&gt; is served at &lt;code&gt;/sitemap.xml&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/sitemap.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;MetadataRoute&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'next'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;getAllPosts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@/lib/posts'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;BASE_URL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://www.yoursite.com'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;sitemap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MetadataRoute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Sitemap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;posts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getAllPosts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;BASE_URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lastModified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${BASE_URL}/pricing`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lastModified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;posts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${BASE_URL}/blog/${post.slug}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;lastModified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;updatedAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;List only final &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; URLs, submit the sitemap in Search Console, and link every important page from at least one other page.&lt;br&gt;
Don't block rendering in robots.txt. Blocking &lt;code&gt;/_next/static/&lt;/code&gt;, CSS or the API routes your pages call stops Google from rendering them. Generate the file with &lt;code&gt;app/robots.ts&lt;/code&gt; and disallow only private areas like &lt;code&gt;/admin/&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Why does Google say "Duplicate without user-selected canonical"?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Several URLs show the same content with no clear canonical, so Google picked one itself.&lt;br&gt;
It's common. According to the HTTP Archive's 2025 Web Almanac, about 1 in 3 pages has no canonical tag, and on roughly 3% of pages JavaScript changes the canonical after rendering, sending Google two conflicting signals.&lt;br&gt;
Typical sources: &lt;code&gt;http&lt;/code&gt; vs &lt;code&gt;https&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;www&lt;/code&gt; vs non-&lt;code&gt;www&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/page&lt;/code&gt; vs &lt;code&gt;/page/&lt;/code&gt;, tracking parameters, an indexable staging domain, and the sneakiest one, a canonical in the root layout. Layout metadata is inherited by every page. We tested it: with &lt;code&gt;alternates: { canonical: '/' }&lt;/code&gt; in the layout, an &lt;code&gt;/about&lt;/code&gt; page with no canonical of its own output &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;link rel="canonical" href="https://www.yoursite.com"/&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, telling Google it's a copy of the homepage.&lt;br&gt;
Set &lt;code&gt;metadataBase&lt;/code&gt; in the layout and the canonical per page:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Metadata&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'next'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/layout.tsx: base URL only, no canonical&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;metadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Metadata&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;metadataBase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://www.yoursite.com'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/pricing/page.tsx: each page declares its own&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;metadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Metadata&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Pricing'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;alternates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;canonical&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'/pricing'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Keep your canonical, sitemap and internal links on the exact same URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Do redirects stop my pages from being indexed?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A permanently redirected URL is supposed to leave the index. The problem is links, sitemaps or canonicals pointing at redirecting URLs.&lt;br&gt;
What Next.js sends (we confirmed each with &lt;code&gt;curl -sI&lt;/code&gt;):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;redirect()&lt;/code&gt; sends &lt;code&gt;307&lt;/code&gt; (temporary)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;permanentRedirect()&lt;/code&gt; sends &lt;code&gt;308&lt;/code&gt; (permanent)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;redirects()&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;next.config&lt;/code&gt; sends &lt;code&gt;308&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;permanent: true&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;307&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;permanent: false&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;
A trailing slash mismatch (&lt;code&gt;/pricing/&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;/pricing&lt;/code&gt;) sends &lt;code&gt;308&lt;/code&gt; (permanent)&lt;br&gt;
Use one trailing-slash style everywhere, collapse redirect chains into one hop, and test locale or auth redirects in &lt;code&gt;proxy.ts&lt;/code&gt; (called &lt;code&gt;middleware.ts&lt;/code&gt; before Next.js 16).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  Why is my Next.js page marked "noindex" or "Soft 404"?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Usually a staging &lt;code&gt;noindex&lt;/code&gt; leaked into production, or missing pages return &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; instead of a real &lt;code&gt;404&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
Leftover &lt;code&gt;noindex&lt;/code&gt;. Tie it to the environment so it can't leak:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/layout.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Metadata&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'next'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// VERCEL_ENV is set by Vercel. On other hosts, use your own env variable.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;isProduction&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;VERCEL_ENV&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'production'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;metadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Metadata&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;metadataBase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://www.yoursite.com'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;robots&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;isProduction&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;follow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;follow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Also check for an &lt;code&gt;X-Robots-Tag: noindex&lt;/code&gt; header.&lt;br&gt;
You also can't remove a &lt;code&gt;noindex&lt;/code&gt; with JavaScript. Google doesn't render pages whose initial HTML contains &lt;code&gt;noindex&lt;/code&gt;, so the removal script never runs (Vercel and MERJ, 2024).&lt;br&gt;
Soft 404s. A single-page app returns &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; and the same &lt;code&gt;index.html&lt;/code&gt; for every URL, even fake ones. In Next.js, call &lt;code&gt;notFound()&lt;/code&gt; when data doesn't exist (as in the product example) and add &lt;code&gt;app/not-found.tsx&lt;/code&gt;. In our test, a missing slug returned a real &lt;code&gt;404&lt;/code&gt;. If streaming has already started (for example, with &lt;code&gt;loading.tsx&lt;/code&gt;), the status stays &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; but Next.js adds a &lt;code&gt;noindex&lt;/code&gt; tag.&lt;br&gt;
For a plain React SPA, return a real &lt;code&gt;404&lt;/code&gt; for unknown routes, or follow Google's SPA guidance and add a &lt;code&gt;noindex&lt;/code&gt; meta tag with JavaScript on your not-found view. Adding works; removing doesn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What's the best way to fix SEO on an existing React app?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Server rendering through Next.js or Astro is the long-term fix. Prerendering can bridge the gap if you can't migrate yet.&lt;br&gt;
Server rendering (Next.js, Remix, Astro): best for sites where search traffic matters. It's a migration, but it fixes everything at once.&lt;br&gt;
Build-time prerendering: good for small sites with fixed pages, but it misses pages built from live data.&lt;br&gt;
Prerendering service: a stopgap for large apps that can't migrate yet. It adds cost, and bots must see the same content as users.&lt;br&gt;
Google's own documentation says dynamic rendering "is a workaround and not a recommended solution" and recommends server-side rendering, static rendering or hydration instead. Migrate the pages that earn search traffic first; logged-in dashboards can stay client-side.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What should I check before requesting indexing?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The page returns &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt;, has its content, title and own canonical in the raw HTML, is in your sitemap and is linked from another page. Check each important URL:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;curl -sIL&lt;/code&gt; shows one &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; with no redirect hops.&lt;br&gt;
Your heading and body text appear in View Source.&lt;br&gt;
A unique title and description are in the raw HTML.&lt;br&gt;
The canonical matches this exact URL.&lt;br&gt;
No &lt;code&gt;noindex&lt;/code&gt; in the HTML or headers, and robots.txt allows the page and its assets.&lt;br&gt;
The URL is in your submitted sitemap and linked with a real &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;a href&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
URL Inspection's Live Test shows your content.&lt;br&gt;
Then click Request Indexing once; repeating it doesn't help.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What if my site is technically fine but still not indexed?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then it's usually content, not code: thin pages, near-duplicates or a new domain Google doesn't trust yet. Merge thin pages, add original information, link from your strongest pages and earn a few external links. Indexing can take days to weeks, and nothing guarantees it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The short version
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Check the raw HTML, match your Search Console status to its cause, then fix rendering, discovery and signals. Server-render what matters, use real links and a sitemap, and keep one canonical URL per page.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Written by the &lt;a href="https://cyberghostsolutions.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Cyber Ghost Solutions&lt;/a&gt; team. We build and optimize websites for UK businesses, with a focus on technical SEO and indexing.&lt;br&gt;
Last updated: November 2026. Code tested on Next.js 16.4 with the App Router.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sources&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://vercel.com/blog/how-google-handles-javascript-throughout-the-indexing-process" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;How Google handles JavaScript throughout the indexing process, Vercel and MERJ (2024)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://vercel.com/blog/the-rise-of-the-ai-crawler" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The rise of the AI crawler, Vercel and MERJ (December 2024)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://almanac.httparchive.org/en/2025/seo" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Web Almanac 2025: SEO chapter, HTTP Archive&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/javascript/dynamic-rendering" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Dynamic rendering as a workaround, Google Search Central&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting a React app from scratch means building every button, input, dialog, and table yourself — and making them all look consistent. MUI (formerly Material-UI) is a component library that gives you all of those pieces, styled with Google's Material Design, ready to drop into your app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Getting started is quick. Install the packages, wrap your app in a theme provider, and use the components:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;TextField&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Card&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Typography&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@mui/material'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;LoginCard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Card&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;maxWidth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;400&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;margin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'40px auto'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;padding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Typography&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;variant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"h5"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;gutterBottom&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Log&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Typography&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
